ys a **dark, maroon red** (like the color of a brick or deep wine). It only looks darker because the oxygen has been used by your body’s cells and the hemoglobin changes shape to a darker form. It’s never anything close to blue. When you get a cut and the dark red blood oozes out and hits the air, it instantly soaks up oxygen from the atmosphere. That changes the hemoglobin from **dark red** (deoxygenated) to **bright cherry red** (oxygenated). So you’re watching a fast color change from dark red to bright red—not from blue to red. So to sum it up simply: Your veins are pale tubes full of dark red blood. The blue color is just a trick of the light in your skin. And when the blood hits air, it turns from dark red to bright red because of oxygen. Does that clear it up a little better?
