e to red." But no blue liquid ever existed; it was simply a dark red becoming a bright red. Think of it like this: If you pour a little dark soy sauce onto a white plate, it looks almost black-brown. If you then mix in a drop of bright ketchup, the color changes. That doesn't mean the soy sauce was blue—it just went from being very dark to very bright. The optical illusion of the vein through the skin masked its real color all along. ### The ultimate proof, again If blood were truly blue inside veins and only turned red upon hitting air, then when a doctor draws blood into a vacuum tube (where it never touches air), the blood in the tube would be blue. But it never is. It's always dark red, even in the sealed tube. That's because the blood itself was always red. So your memory is completely accurate—you did see a "blue vein," and you did see blood that immediately looked red. But the blue vein was an illusion covering up dark red blood, and the red you saw was just the brightening of that dark red. You never saw a blue liquid. I hope that explanation makes sense of your experience. It's a testament to how powerful that optical trick really is.
